[[Magic]] is performed by drawing Casting Seals, otherwise known as spells. Spells are typically composed of one or more [[sigils]] and [[signs]], which control the spell's behaviour, as well as a surrounding ring, which once fully connected serves to activate the spell.<ref name=":0" /> Closing an empty spell ring with nothing inside results in a large shockwave, destroying the seal in the process.{{Ref|m|46|7}} These symbols must be drawn using [[Woodcruor]] of some kind.<ref name=":0">''Witch Hat Atelier'' Manga: [[Chapter 3]]</ref>

Composition and accuracy plays a large part in the outcome of the spell,{{Ref|m|12|10}} with smaller or messier seals being weaker or shorter lived than larger or neater ones.{{Ref|m|1|48}} The same principle applies to the signs as well.<ref name=":0" /> Spells will have a fleeting effect or may even fail to activate completely if the ring is not circular enough.{{Ref|m|81|15-16}} 

Many small seals can be linked together to replicate the strength a larger one, this can be useful in situations where drawing a large seal accurately would be impractical or impossible, or to conceal the spell for use in a [[contraption]].{{Ref|m|10|16}}

Unfortunately, the designs of some seals have been somewhat inconsistent, so a few spells have different spell construction now than they did earlier on in the story. (This is especially prevalent in the [[Volume 1|first]] and [[Volume 2|second]] volume chapters). One example of this is how the Wall Breaker seals early on in the manga have a levitation sign in place of an earth sigil,{{Ref|m|7|17}} though it was somewhat fixed for the volume release. Due to this and similar cases, if a seal has different forms at different points in the story, the newer version of that seal will take precedence and become the official form of that seal.

Spells are categorized by common sigils and/or effects. Each spell has its own page. If you would like to learn more about a specific spell, click on its name to be redirected to that spell's page.

Note: Occasionally, the term sigil is used to refer to Casting Seals in their entirety, excluding the ring.{{Ref|m|35|6}}{{Ref|m|46|10}}{{Ref|a|6|18:12-18:18}} Differentiating between the two meanings requires looking at the source material contextually. When this discrepancy arises, it will be noted on the relevant pages.
